<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Knin</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Knin is a town in the Dalmatian hinterland of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>, on the Krka river, beneath a large hilltop fortress. It was one of the royal seats of the medieval Croatian kings and the seat of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>. Later an Ottoman and then a Venetian fortress, it was the capital of the self-proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/republic-of-serbian-krajina">Republic of Serbian Krajina</a> from 1991 until Croatian forces retook it in Operation Storm in 1995.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/knin.jpg" alt="Knin" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CroatianPalpatine · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Knin_25.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Knin lies in the northern Dalmatian hinterland of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>, 56 kilometres east of Šibenik, on the foothills of the Dinaric Alps, where the Krka river rises on the outskirts of the town. The fortress stands on these foothills. The Roman town of Burnum, a military camp in the 1st century BCE, lay about 18 kilometres away, and its amphitheatre, built in 77 CE under Vespasian, could hold 8,000 people. The villages of Biskupija and Kapitul nearby are archaeological sites of the 10th century with churches, graves and inscriptions of medieval Croatian culture.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The settlement grew on the Spas hill and later formed the castle. A monastery of Saint Bartholomew was built at Kapitul in the 9th century, in the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trpimir-i">Trpimir I</a>. Knin is first mentioned in the 10th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/de-administrando-imperio">De Administrando Imperio</a> as the centre of a county and one of the populated towns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>. Around 1040 the Croatian kings had a seat of the royal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> established in the nearby village of Biskupija, and the bishops served at court until 1102, when Croatia entered its union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>.</p><p>Knin fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in 1522 and later became the centre of the Sanjak of Kırka, founded in 1574. The Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> briefly occupied it in 1648 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cretan-war-1645-1669">Cretan War</a> and took it again in 1688. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franciscans">Franciscans</a> built a monastery and church there in 1708, and a siege by the Ottomans in 1715 was repulsed. Knin passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a> in 1797 under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-campo-formio">Treaty of Campo Formio</a>, was part of the French Illyrian Provinces from 1809, and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> in 1813. By the end of the 19th century it had become an important commercial, road and railway centre, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lika">Lika</a> railway linked it with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagreb">Zagreb</a> in 1925.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the area lay in the Italian occupation zone, administered by the Independent State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>, and was the scene of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ustase">Ustaša</a> campaign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a> against the local Serb population. Before the Croatian War of Independence, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> made up 87 percent of the municipality and 79 percent of the town. During the war most of the non-Serb population was displaced, and in its last days most Serbs left, fearing the incoming Croatian forces. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-court-of-justice">International Court of Justice</a> stated in 2015 that a substantial part of the Serb population fled as a direct consequence of military actions.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Knin is a city in Šibenik-Knin County and an important junction of the rail and road routes between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagreb">Zagreb</a> and Split, with lines running on to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zadar">Zadar</a> and Šibenik. The 2021 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 11,755 people in the municipality and 8,317 in the town itself. It has a hot-summer climate modified by the nearby mountains.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Knin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ban Petar Berislavić won at Dubica in 1513 but was killed in 1520, <b>Knin</b> fell on 29 May 1522, and Petar Kružić defended Klis for almost 25 years.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-croatia-1102-1526">Kingdom of Croatia (1102-1526) · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Kings ruled with a council of nobles and bans, from royal seats such as <b>Knin</b>, Biograd na Moru and Nin.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-croatia-925-1102">Kingdom of Croatia (925-1102)</a></li></ul></section>
